
Git is a free, open-source distributed version control system used to track changes in source code during software development. It was created by Linus Torvalds in 2005 to help manage the development of the Linux kernel.

Key Features
Distributed Development
Every developer has a complete copy of the repository, including its full history.
Work can continue even without an internet connection.
Version Control
Tracks every change made to files.
Allows you to view, compare, and restore previous versions.
Branching and Merging
Create separate branches to develop new features or fix bugs without affecting the main code.
Merge branches efficiently when changes are ready.
Fast Performance
Operations like committing, branching, and merging are designed to be quick and efficient.
Data Integrity
Git uses cryptographic hashing (SHA) to uniquely identify commits and ensure data has not been corrupted.
Collaboration
Multiple developers can work on the same project simultaneously.
Supports workflows using remote repositories (such as those hosted on GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket).
Staging Area (Index)
Lets you choose which changes to include in the next commit.
Enables creating well-organized and meaningful commits.
Commit History
Maintains a detailed history of changes, including who made each change, when, and why (through commit messages).
Backup and Recovery
Since every clone contains the full repository history, data recovery is easier if a remote repository is lost.
Open Source and Cross-Platform
Free to use and works on Windows, macOS, and Linux.
Summary

