Summary

The key features of Git are:

Distributed version control
Fast performance
Branching and merging
Version tracking
Data integrity
Collaboration support
Staging area
Complete commit history
Backup and recovery
Cross-platform and open sourceV




Version tracking — records every change made to files, so you can revert to any previous version at any time.



Branching and merging — lets you create separate "branches" to work on new features or fixes without affecting the main codebase, then merge them 
Remote hosting — store repos online, accessible from anywhere, with push/pull to sync local and remote copies.
Pull Requests (PRs) — propose changes from one branch/fork into another, with code review, comments, and approval workflows before merging.
Issues — track bugs, tasks, and feature requests, with labels, assignees, and milestones.
GitHub Actions — built-in CI/CD automation (run tests, build, deploy) triggered by events like pushes or PRs.
Forking — copy someone else's repo into your account to experiment or contribute back via PR.
GitHub Pages — free static site hosting directly from a repo.
Wikis & README — documentation built into each repo.
Access control — manage collaborators, teams, and permissions (public/private repos, organizations).
Code review tools — inline comments, diffs, suggested changes, required reviewers.
Security tools — Dependabot (dependency vulnerability alerts), secret scanning, code scanning.
Projects/boards — Kanban-style boards for tracking work.
GitHub Marketplace — third-party integrations and apps.
Codespaces — cloud-based dev environments launched directly from a repo.
